1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,000
They're the products of a multi-billion dollar

2
00:00:03,001 --> 00:00:08,840
industry that started out making cures for what ailed you,

3
00:00:08,841 --> 00:00:11,240
mass produced and microbrewed.

4
00:00:11,241 --> 00:00:13,040
We're the only people commercially

5
00:00:13,041 --> 00:00:15,040
who brew a soda that old fashioned way.

6
00:00:15,041 --> 00:00:16,960
That's our claim to fame.

7
00:00:16,961 --> 00:00:19,760
A tiny seed gives some of them more than twice

8
00:00:19,761 --> 00:00:23,160
the caffeine boost of a cup of coffee,

9
00:00:23,161 --> 00:00:26,160
while the key to formulating others is sending sweat

10
00:00:26,161 --> 00:00:29,160
through a centrifuge.

11
00:00:29,161 --> 00:00:33,660
It's time to twist your top, pour a glass, and chill out.

12
00:00:33,760 --> 00:00:37,160
Now, soft drinks on Modern Marvels.

13
00:00:53,460 --> 00:00:56,160
Nearly 15 billion gallons of soft drinks

14
00:00:56,260 --> 00:00:59,160
quench the thirsts of Americans each year.

15
00:00:59,161 --> 00:01:03,160
That's nearly the combined volume of coffee, beer,

16
00:01:03,260 --> 00:01:06,760
and bottled water consumed annually in the US.

17
00:01:08,840 --> 00:01:11,400
A host of flavors, eye-catching containers,

18
00:01:11,500 --> 00:01:15,240
and claims of invigoration continually entice us

19
00:01:15,340 --> 00:01:18,040
to gulp away.

20
00:01:18,140 --> 00:01:20,780
What exactly are soft drinks?

21
00:01:20,880 --> 00:01:24,360
Simply put, they're non-alcoholic liquid refreshments.

22
00:01:24,460 --> 00:01:27,080
But it's not quite that simple.

23
00:01:27,081 --> 00:01:29,180
Are coffees and teas soft drinks?

24
00:01:29,280 --> 00:01:30,380
No.

25
00:01:30,480 --> 00:01:31,660
Bottled water?

26
00:01:31,760 --> 00:01:32,860
Nope.

27
00:01:32,960 --> 00:01:34,020
Milk?

28
00:01:34,120 --> 00:01:35,160
Ditto.

29
00:01:35,260 --> 00:01:36,820
How about fruit juices?

30
00:01:36,920 --> 00:01:39,500
Maybe to some, but we'll save those for another show.

31
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:41,000
I think in the United States, people

32
00:01:41,100 --> 00:01:43,800
tend to think of a soft drink more in the carbonated sense,

33
00:01:43,900 --> 00:01:45,800
so carbonated soft drinks.

34
00:01:45,900 --> 00:01:47,560
But in reality, it's broader than that.

35
00:01:47,660 --> 00:01:50,040
The carbonated soft drink category is far and away

36
00:01:50,140 --> 00:01:52,740
the largest beverage category in the US.

37
00:01:52,840 --> 00:01:55,440
People drink more carbonated soft drinks

38
00:01:55,441 --> 00:01:56,940
than any other beverage.

39
00:02:04,240 --> 00:02:07,180
U.S. soda makers produce enough carbonated soft drinks

40
00:02:07,280 --> 00:02:10,580
to supply each American with more than 50 gallons per year.

41
00:02:13,700 --> 00:02:15,520
It's old news that the top two companies

42
00:02:15,620 --> 00:02:20,440
are Coca-Cola and Pepsi, with brands and logos

43
00:02:20,540 --> 00:02:21,460
known worldwide.

44
00:02:21,461 --> 00:02:24,360
worldwide.

45
00:02:24,460 --> 00:02:28,200
But number three, with 18% of the market,

46
00:02:28,300 --> 00:02:31,600
that's the Dr. Pepper Snapple Group.

47
00:02:31,700 --> 00:02:34,680
It bottles six of the top 10 non-cola carbonated soft

48
00:02:34,780 --> 00:02:40,580
drinks, led naturally by Dr. Pepper.

49
00:02:40,680 --> 00:02:42,480
Its signature taste is actually a blend

50
00:02:42,580 --> 00:02:43,920
of a multiplicity of flavors.

51
00:02:46,960 --> 00:02:49,420
There's 23 flavors, which has made it more unique than most

52
00:02:49,520 --> 00:02:50,420
soft drinks.

53
00:02:50,421 --> 00:02:53,100
It doesn't have kind of an easily identifiable flavor.

54
00:02:53,320 --> 00:02:55,560
It is truly a flavored drink that is unlike colas

55
00:02:55,561 --> 00:02:58,380
and unlike a specific flavor like an orange or a grape.

56
00:02:59,940 --> 00:03:01,920
But what are those 23 flavors?

57
00:03:02,720 --> 00:03:04,660
Not allowed to share the 23 flavors.

58
00:03:04,920 --> 00:03:06,300
It is a closely held secret.

59
00:03:06,860 --> 00:03:08,720
We actually have it locked in a vault

60
00:03:08,721 --> 00:03:10,440
in our headquarters in Plano, Texas.

61
00:03:10,740 --> 00:03:12,280
I think maybe seven people actually know

62
00:03:12,281 --> 00:03:13,780
what all the 23 flavors are.

63
00:03:15,160 --> 00:03:17,760
There is one thing the company is willing to share with us.

64
00:03:17,761 --> 00:03:22,560
Howard's plant in Irving, Texas, produces approximately 70 million

65
00:03:22,660 --> 00:03:26,160
cases of soft drinks a year.

66
00:03:26,260 --> 00:03:30,100
That's enough soft drinks to fill more than 250 Olympic-sized

67
00:03:30,200 --> 00:03:32,080
swimming pools.

68
00:03:32,180 --> 00:03:34,480
The Irving Manufacturing Facility is the largest

69
00:03:34,580 --> 00:03:36,640
in the Dr. Pepper's Napple Group system.

70
00:03:36,740 --> 00:03:38,440
It is one of 24 plants.

71
00:03:38,540 --> 00:03:40,880
It's approximately 1 million square feet.

72
00:03:40,980 --> 00:03:45,380
300 manufactured employees run the facility.

73
00:03:45,381 --> 00:03:48,580
The soda-making process starts with the main ingredient,

74
00:03:48,680 --> 00:03:52,680
comprising 99% of every soft drink.

75
00:03:52,780 --> 00:03:55,320
The heart of any soft drink manufacturing facility

76
00:03:55,420 --> 00:03:56,320
is water.

77
00:03:56,420 --> 00:04:00,420
We use a lot of it, upwards of a million gallons a day.

78
00:04:00,520 --> 00:04:04,440
Carbon filter towers, reverse osmosis units,

79
00:04:04,540 --> 00:04:07,440
and UV disinfection rays decontaminate and purify the

80
00:04:07,540 --> 00:04:10,340
water before the next key ingredient is added.

81
00:04:10,341 --> 00:04:16,320
added, the stuff that gives soft drinks their sweet flavor,

82
00:04:16,420 --> 00:04:19,620
and caffeinated ones their kick, the syrup.

83
00:04:19,720 --> 00:04:21,320
We're here at syrup room number one today,

84
00:04:21,420 --> 00:04:25,160
where we're going to produce Canada Dry Ginger Ale syrup.

85
00:04:25,260 --> 00:04:28,960
In syrup preparation, we combine concentrate sweetener

86
00:04:29,060 --> 00:04:31,860
and treated water.

87
00:04:31,960 --> 00:04:33,660
The all-important sweetener in the mix

88
00:04:33,760 --> 00:04:35,860
isn't the sugar you use at home.

89
00:04:35,960 --> 00:04:40,340
It's high fructose corn syrup 55, or HFCS 55.

90
00:04:40,341 --> 00:04:44,180
Sucrose, or table sugar, is 50% fructose,

91
00:04:44,280 --> 00:04:48,580
a simple sugar found in fruits, and 50% glucose,

92
00:04:48,680 --> 00:04:50,580
an energy-supplying simple sugar,

93
00:04:50,680 --> 00:04:52,980
essential for human metabolism.

94
00:04:53,080 --> 00:05:00,000
HFCS 55 is 55% fructose and 45% glucose.

95
00:05:00,100 --> 00:05:02,900
The corn-based sweetener has some advantages over sugar

96
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:05,140
for soft drink makers.

97
00:05:05,240 --> 00:05:07,600
High fructose corn syrup, or HFCS,

98
00:05:07,601 --> 00:05:11,800
became industry standard as a sweetener in the mid-'80s

99
00:05:11,900 --> 00:05:14,940
and allows us to produce more and do it at a relatively

100
00:05:15,040 --> 00:05:16,440
good price for consumers.

101
00:05:18,940 --> 00:05:21,440
Pipes carry the combination of syrup and water

102
00:05:21,540 --> 00:05:24,480
to the filling room, where it awaits an instant injection

103
00:05:24,580 --> 00:05:30,460
of the gas that puts the fizz in our soda, carbon dioxide.

104
00:05:30,560 --> 00:05:34,360
Here you have treated water and syrup mixed together

105
00:05:34,361 --> 00:05:37,560
in a mixing chamber at a 5 to 1 ratio.

106
00:05:37,660 --> 00:05:39,900
Right now, we're running approximately 60 gallons

107
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:42,600
a minute of water and approximately 12 gallons

108
00:05:42,700 --> 00:05:44,000
a minute of syrup.

109
00:05:44,100 --> 00:05:47,600
We carbonate it 35 degrees to ensure the CO2 gets

110
00:05:47,700 --> 00:05:49,100
into solution.

111
00:05:49,200 --> 00:05:50,840
Carbonating at warmer temperatures

112
00:05:50,940 --> 00:05:52,880
would cause the bubbles to dissipate

113
00:05:52,980 --> 00:05:56,440
and make the soda go flat.

114
00:05:56,540 --> 00:05:58,120
The process at the filling stations

115
00:05:58,220 --> 00:06:01,780
help preserve the fids as the soda is injected into cans

116
00:06:01,781 --> 00:06:04,280
cans or bottles that are then rapidly sealed.

117
00:06:08,280 --> 00:06:09,780
Sweet, busy beverages like these

118
00:06:09,880 --> 00:06:12,220
have become an iconic part of the cultural landscape.

119
00:06:15,620 --> 00:06:18,360
Carbonated softener is a huge part of American culture

120
00:06:18,460 --> 00:06:21,160
and much more popular here than a lot of markets

121
00:06:21,260 --> 00:06:22,500
around the world.

122
00:06:22,600 --> 00:06:25,900
There's a lot of history steeped in tradition.

123
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:28,400
History's earliest mentions of carbonated liquids

124
00:06:28,401 --> 00:06:31,640
to the ancient Greeks and Romans.

125
00:06:31,740 --> 00:06:35,040
They prized natural mineral water for its health benefits,

126
00:06:35,140 --> 00:06:36,640
but they didn't drink it.

127
00:06:36,740 --> 00:06:39,140
They bathed in it.

128
00:06:39,240 --> 00:06:43,140
Europeans revived that tradition in the 18th and 19th centuries,

129
00:06:43,240 --> 00:06:46,260
except they also started bottling and imbibing it.

130
00:06:49,860 --> 00:06:52,960
The commercial carbonated soft drink tradition as we know it

131
00:06:53,060 --> 00:06:54,960
began in the late 19th century.

132
00:06:58,400 --> 00:07:01,440
most brands originated as the elixirs of pharmacists

133
00:07:01,540 --> 00:07:04,540
at neighborhood drugstores.

134
00:07:04,640 --> 00:07:09,540
They were marketed not only as refreshments, but cure-alls.

135
00:07:09,640 --> 00:07:11,940
Dr. Pepper led the way.

136
00:07:12,040 --> 00:07:16,360
It was introduced in 1885 in Waco, Texas.

137
00:07:16,460 --> 00:07:21,660
Doc Alderton invented it in Morrison's Corner Drugstore.

138
00:07:21,760 --> 00:07:24,300
And really, it gained world popularity in 1904

139
00:07:24,400 --> 00:07:26,760
at the World's Fair in St. Louis,

140
00:07:26,761 --> 00:07:30,460
with the hot dog that was being introduced at the same time.

141
00:07:30,560 --> 00:07:34,540
Coca-Cola came along in 1886 when Atlanta, Georgia,

142
00:07:34,640 --> 00:07:39,740
pharmacist Dr. John Pemberton produced its first syrup.

143
00:07:39,840 --> 00:07:43,640
Carbonated water was added at the soda fountain,

144
00:07:43,740 --> 00:07:46,880
and the tonic sold for 5 cents a glass.

145
00:07:46,980 --> 00:07:52,020
Sales that first year averaged nine drinks per day.

146
00:07:52,120 --> 00:07:54,360
You may have heard the story about Coca-Cola's original

147
00:07:54,361 --> 00:07:57,700
recipe containing coca leaves, the plant from which cocaine

148
00:07:57,800 --> 00:07:58,500
is derived.

149
00:08:01,060 --> 00:08:03,000
The stimulating leaf was thought to be a remedy

150
00:08:03,100 --> 00:08:04,000
for various ailments.

151
00:08:06,940 --> 00:08:10,100
After continuous public outcry over the dangers of the drug,

152
00:08:10,200 --> 00:08:12,580
the cola maker quietly removed it from its recipe

153
00:08:12,680 --> 00:08:14,020
in the early 1900s.

154
00:08:17,720 --> 00:08:21,720
The signature ingredient in Pepsi, which debuted in 1893,

155
00:08:21,721 --> 00:08:26,160
was also the inspiration for the cola's name, pepsin,

156
00:08:26,260 --> 00:08:28,860
an enzyme that aids with digestion.

157
00:08:28,960 --> 00:08:31,500
But initially, it was dubbed Brad's drink,

158
00:08:31,600 --> 00:08:36,340
after the pharmacist who invented it, Caleb Bradham.

159
00:08:36,440 --> 00:08:39,340
Today, Dr. Pepper has its loyal following.

160
00:08:39,440 --> 00:08:43,240
But Coke and Pepsi continue to dominate the industry.

161
00:08:43,340 --> 00:08:45,840
One of the reasons Coke and Pepsi are so successful

162
00:08:45,940 --> 00:08:48,480
is because they have cola brands.

163
00:08:48,481 --> 00:08:52,080
Colas are more than half of the carbonated soft drink,

164
00:08:52,180 --> 00:08:53,660
that flavor component.

165
00:08:53,760 --> 00:08:55,280
Americans love colas.

166
00:08:55,380 --> 00:08:58,460
And so those two brands have kind of slugged it out

167
00:08:58,560 --> 00:09:03,300
for dominance in the carbonated soft drink category.

168
00:09:03,400 --> 00:09:05,460
But there's more at stake in the soft drink industry

169
00:09:05,560 --> 00:09:08,140
than this long-running rivalry.

170
00:09:08,240 --> 00:09:11,140
Many health-conscious consumers are turning their backs

171
00:09:11,240 --> 00:09:14,900
on popular brands, which may contain artificial flavors,

172
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:16,540
colors, and preservatives.

173
00:09:18,480 --> 00:09:21,320
Hanson's natural sodas made near Los Angeles

174
00:09:21,420 --> 00:09:25,420
are among the brands offering an alternative.

175
00:09:25,520 --> 00:09:29,760
In 2006 and in 2007, we saw about a 2% decline

176
00:09:29,860 --> 00:09:31,800
in overall mainstream soda sales.

177
00:09:31,900 --> 00:09:33,800
But during that same time, natural sodas

178
00:09:33,900 --> 00:09:35,700
like Hanson's natural soda actually

179
00:09:35,800 --> 00:09:38,700
showed an increase in grocery store sales of about 18%

180
00:09:38,800 --> 00:09:40,100
to 20% a year.

181
00:09:40,200 --> 00:09:42,700
It's really the moms who started the trend

182
00:09:42,800 --> 00:09:44,140
towards natural sodas.

183
00:09:44,240 --> 00:09:46,240
So they were starting to really be concerned

184
00:09:46,241 --> 00:09:48,200
really be concerned about preservatives

185
00:09:48,201 --> 00:09:52,180
and artificial colors, artificial flavors.

186
00:09:52,181 --> 00:09:55,840
The Hansen's natural soda would be made with a natural flavor.

187
00:09:55,841 --> 00:09:59,420
And a natural flavor is actually derived from the essence

188
00:09:59,421 --> 00:10:02,000
that is extracted from the actual fruit,

189
00:10:02,001 --> 00:10:04,180
as opposed to an artificial flavor,

190
00:10:04,181 --> 00:10:06,560
which is a chemically synthesized compound.

191
00:10:06,561 --> 00:10:10,520
For example, the Hansen's mandarin lime natural flavor

192
00:10:10,521 --> 00:10:14,100
would actually have essences derived from Yucatan mandarins

193
00:10:14,101 --> 00:10:18,000
and Kolyma limes, as opposed to, say, an orange soda

194
00:10:18,100 --> 00:10:21,500
that's made with an artificial flavor.

195
00:10:21,600 --> 00:10:24,380
Until 2008, Hansen's natural sodas

196
00:10:24,480 --> 00:10:26,740
contained the same crucial sweetener.

197
00:10:26,840 --> 00:10:32,180
It's in Coke, Pepsi, Dr. Pepper, and most popular brands,

198
00:10:32,280 --> 00:10:35,580
high fructose corn syrup.

199
00:10:35,680 --> 00:10:38,960
The scientific jury is still out concerning whether HFCS

200
00:10:39,060 --> 00:10:42,800
contributes to obesity more than pure cane sugar.

201
00:10:42,801 --> 00:10:45,100
But Hansen's decided to make the change to sugar

202
00:10:45,200 --> 00:10:48,400
based on appeals from its customers.

203
00:10:48,500 --> 00:10:51,140
For us, it's all about reacting to what the consumers

204
00:10:51,240 --> 00:10:54,140
and the retailers have asked us to do.

205
00:10:54,240 --> 00:10:56,240
The main piece of equipment Hansen's required

206
00:10:56,340 --> 00:10:59,560
to make the switch was this liquid sucrose holding tank.

207
00:11:03,860 --> 00:11:06,560
Two to three times a day, the sugar supplier

208
00:11:06,660 --> 00:11:11,500
fills the tank with 5,000 gallons.

209
00:11:11,501 --> 00:11:15,100
It's just one of Hansen's ingredients on a short list.

210
00:11:15,200 --> 00:11:17,500
Rather than have maybe 40 different ingredients,

211
00:11:17,600 --> 00:11:21,940
like red dye number 40, sodium benzoate, potassium sorbate,

212
00:11:22,040 --> 00:11:25,080
Hansen's natural soda just has these four ingredients,

213
00:11:25,180 --> 00:11:30,120
triple filtered purified water, cane sugar, citric acid,

214
00:11:30,220 --> 00:11:34,760
and pure fruit flavor extract.

215
00:11:34,860 --> 00:11:36,800
But the key ingredient in the fastest growing

216
00:11:36,900 --> 00:11:39,100
niche of the soft drink market is caffeine.

217
00:11:41,500 --> 00:11:45,540
And one can might contain more caffeine than a cup of coffee.

218
00:11:49,440 --> 00:11:50,940
20 to 10 for Walsh and A.

219
00:11:54,840 --> 00:11:57,540
Soft drinks are designed to quench our thirst,

220
00:11:57,640 --> 00:12:00,720
satisfy our sweet tooth, and since the introduction

221
00:12:00,820 --> 00:12:06,560
of sports drinks in 1965, refuel our bodies.

222
00:12:06,660 --> 00:12:09,960
For decades, top athletes, including two-time Olympic beach

223
00:12:09,961 --> 00:12:13,160
volleyball gold medalists Carrie Walsh and Misty May

224
00:12:13,260 --> 00:12:16,760
Traynor have allied themselves with a sports drink leader,

225
00:12:16,860 --> 00:12:17,660
Gatorade.

226
00:12:21,900 --> 00:12:24,100
And they're even willing to donate their sweat

227
00:12:24,200 --> 00:12:27,520
to Gatorade scientists using these special patches.

228
00:12:30,420 --> 00:12:34,420
Whether in a real world environment like this,

229
00:12:34,520 --> 00:12:38,220
or here at the Gatorade Sports Science Institute outside

230
00:12:38,221 --> 00:12:44,220
The serious business of sports drink research sweats the details.

231
00:12:44,320 --> 00:12:47,100
This rapidly growing sector of the soft drink market

232
00:12:47,200 --> 00:12:52,160
accounts for nearly 3% of all beverages purchased in the US.

233
00:12:52,260 --> 00:12:55,100
And Gatorade has been number one since the beginning.

234
00:12:55,200 --> 00:12:58,780
Gatorade is the biggest sports drink brand in the United States.

235
00:12:58,880 --> 00:13:02,780
It accounts for about 80% of the sports drink market.

236
00:13:02,880 --> 00:13:05,580
Powerade would be the second biggest brand,

237
00:13:05,581 --> 00:13:08,580
probably about 15% or 16% of the market.

238
00:13:08,680 --> 00:13:10,060
And when you add those two together,

239
00:13:10,160 --> 00:13:13,180
that doesn't leave much room for anybody else.

240
00:13:13,280 --> 00:13:15,220
In its effort to stay on top,

241
00:13:15,320 --> 00:13:17,820
Gatorade has made a science of the task of replenishing

242
00:13:17,920 --> 00:13:21,900
what the body loses during physical activity.

243
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:23,640
The Gatorade Sports Science Institute

244
00:13:23,740 --> 00:13:26,240
is responsible for showing that Gatorade works,

245
00:13:26,340 --> 00:13:27,940
looking at it in different situations.

246
00:13:28,040 --> 00:13:30,280
We're here to take a look at how we can help the athlete

247
00:13:30,380 --> 00:13:32,900
in terms of what they need to put into their body

248
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:34,940
to stay hydrated.

249
00:13:34,941 --> 00:13:38,940
To stay hydrated, the body needs to replace electrolytes

250
00:13:39,040 --> 00:13:41,680
lost through sweating.

251
00:13:41,780 --> 00:13:45,020
Electrolytes are charged minerals, sodium, potassium,

252
00:13:45,120 --> 00:13:49,680
chloride, that are in our muscle and nerve cells,

253
00:13:49,780 --> 00:13:53,260
and they help transport electrical activity from the brain

254
00:13:53,360 --> 00:13:55,960
to the muscle and stimulate the muscles to contract.

255
00:13:56,060 --> 00:13:58,800
So you can exert the force and move or kick the ball,

256
00:13:58,900 --> 00:14:03,000
whatever you want to do in terms of sports and activity.

257
00:14:03,001 --> 00:14:05,700
Collecting sweat enables Gatorade scientists

258
00:14:05,800 --> 00:14:09,380
to measure an athlete's electrolyte depletion.

259
00:14:09,480 --> 00:14:11,800
This will help them determine the right amount of electrolytes

260
00:14:11,900 --> 00:14:15,120
to put into their various Gatorade formulas.

261
00:14:15,220 --> 00:14:17,120
The idea of these sweat tests is for the athlete

262
00:14:17,220 --> 00:14:19,980
to work out at the intensity that they would typically do,

263
00:14:20,080 --> 00:14:21,880
either in training or competition.

264
00:14:21,980 --> 00:14:26,060
Your sweating rate changes with intensity, air temperature,

265
00:14:26,160 --> 00:14:28,900
your fitness level, the amount of clothing you're wearing.

266
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:31,060
So when an athlete comes into our lab,

267
00:14:31,061 --> 00:14:33,060
we try to control all those factors

268
00:14:33,160 --> 00:14:35,060
and make them as similar to their training

269
00:14:35,160 --> 00:14:38,160
or competition as possible.

270
00:14:38,260 --> 00:14:40,060
It's getting another heart rate here.

271
00:14:40,160 --> 00:14:42,700
Make sure you're working.

272
00:14:42,800 --> 00:14:45,400
Once the patches become saturated with sweat,

273
00:14:45,500 --> 00:14:48,720
researchers take them for a spin in a centrifuge.

274
00:14:48,820 --> 00:14:51,120
And it's this spinning that actually extracts

275
00:14:51,220 --> 00:14:54,920
the fluid from these patches, and it's that fluid

276
00:14:55,020 --> 00:14:57,920
that we measure to determine electrolyte concentration.

277
00:15:01,060 --> 00:15:06,060
So now that our spinning is complete,

278
00:15:06,061 --> 00:15:10,060
what we get is the sweat that we use.

279
00:15:10,061 --> 00:15:14,060
Look, this will tell us exactly how much sodium and potassium

280
00:15:14,061 --> 00:15:17,060
is in the athlete's sweat.

281
00:15:17,061 --> 00:15:20,060
Beyond electrolytes, the other thing the body needs

282
00:15:20,061 --> 00:15:24,060
to keep moving is carbohydrates.

283
00:15:24,061 --> 00:15:26,060
They're the second key ingredient in Gatorade.

284
00:15:26,061 --> 00:15:29,060
Carbohydrates are the primary fuel the body uses.

285
00:15:29,061 --> 00:15:30,860
the primary fuel the body uses.

286
00:15:30,960 --> 00:15:36,100
The more intense you exercise, the more your body relies

287
00:15:36,200 --> 00:15:39,900
exclusively on carbohydrate.

288
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:41,800
This concoction of carbs and electrolytes

289
00:15:41,900 --> 00:15:47,420
traces its origins to the mid-1960s.

290
00:15:47,520 --> 00:15:50,620
The brainchild of University of Florida researchers led by Dr.

291
00:15:50,720 --> 00:15:53,420
Robert Cade.

292
00:15:53,520 --> 00:15:57,020
Their first test subjects, the Florida Gators football team.

293
00:15:57,021 --> 00:15:58,260
with heat stress.

294
00:15:58,360 --> 00:16:01,120
The medical professors at the university approached

295
00:16:01,220 --> 00:16:03,600
the team about developing something that would help them

296
00:16:03,700 --> 00:16:07,600
withstand the stress, the dehydration, and fatigue.

297
00:16:07,700 --> 00:16:12,400
And so they concocted this formula for Gatorade.

298
00:16:12,500 --> 00:16:15,440
After they started guzzling Gatorade on the sidelines,

299
00:16:15,540 --> 00:16:19,540
the Gators went from a 7-4 record in 1965

300
00:16:19,640 --> 00:16:23,380
to an Orange Bowl championship two years later.

301
00:16:23,480 --> 00:16:26,120
Many credited Gatorade with improving the team's overall

302
00:16:26,121 --> 00:16:27,120
overall endurance.

303
00:16:30,560 --> 00:16:33,120
Another type of soft drink that appeals to those seeking a

304
00:16:33,220 --> 00:16:35,620
stimulating boost is the energy drink.

305
00:16:39,760 --> 00:16:43,780
Energy drinks may contain up to 80 milligrams or more of caffeine,

306
00:16:43,880 --> 00:16:47,480
about as much as a cup of coffee, and double that of some sodas.

307
00:16:50,480 --> 00:16:54,520
Most brands also include Guaraná, a South American plant

308
00:16:54,521 --> 00:16:56,820
that contain up to three times the amount of caffeine

309
00:16:56,920 --> 00:17:00,920
as a coffee bean, and a synthetic version of taurine,

310
00:17:01,020 --> 00:17:04,600
a natural amino acid that assists with digestion.

311
00:17:04,700 --> 00:17:06,540
Energy drinks are a recent phenomenon.

312
00:17:06,640 --> 00:17:09,300
It's a category that's only about 10 years old,

313
00:17:09,400 --> 00:17:12,340
coming into the US marketplace in the mid-'90s

314
00:17:12,440 --> 00:17:16,040
and continuing today, where the category has had immense growth,

315
00:17:16,140 --> 00:17:19,720
about 500% over the last five years.

316
00:17:19,820 --> 00:17:23,120
In 2007, US energy drink sales reached

317
00:17:23,121 --> 00:17:26,460
reached more than $7 billion.

318
00:17:26,560 --> 00:17:28,260
An increasing percentage of those sales

319
00:17:28,360 --> 00:17:33,260
come from places like Temple, a San Francisco nightclub,

320
00:17:33,360 --> 00:17:37,300
where crowds line up for a night of mingling, dancing,

321
00:17:37,400 --> 00:17:39,740
and drinking.

322
00:17:39,840 --> 00:17:41,900
I think that energy drinks, if they

323
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:43,680
are going to be successful, they almost

324
00:17:43,780 --> 00:17:46,080
have to be introduced in a nightclub or bar setting.

325
00:17:46,180 --> 00:17:47,780
And then for this gentleman here,

326
00:17:47,880 --> 00:17:50,720
we have four great news and energy drinks.

327
00:17:50,820 --> 00:17:53,080
It gives them the opportunity to expose the energy

328
00:17:53,120 --> 00:17:55,960
product to a lot of people who then might later, when they go

329
00:17:55,961 --> 00:17:58,620
to the grocery store, see that product that they were served in

330
00:17:58,621 --> 00:18:01,320
the nightclub that they like, and then they're more likely to

331
00:18:01,321 --> 00:18:02,460
buy it.

332
00:18:02,461 --> 00:18:06,660
Nightclub and bar sales really drive the retail sales.

333
00:18:06,661 --> 00:18:09,160
One brand of energy drinks partnering with Temple is

334
00:18:09,161 --> 00:18:13,280
Jet Set, a company hoping to establish itself in the industry.

335
00:18:17,280 --> 00:18:19,980
While Jet Set provides consumers with the standard fruity

336
00:18:19,981 --> 00:18:21,220
energy drink.

337
00:18:21,320 --> 00:18:25,760
It also offers classic mixers with a contemporary kick.

338
00:18:25,860 --> 00:18:29,020
We make a club soda, a tonic water, and a ginger ale.

339
00:18:29,120 --> 00:18:33,020
It all can be mixed with whatever spirit you prefer.

340
00:18:33,120 --> 00:18:35,300
If you enjoy drinking gin and tonics,

341
00:18:35,400 --> 00:18:37,060
you can have a gin and jet set tonic,

342
00:18:37,160 --> 00:18:39,260
and it gives you that same functional kick

343
00:18:39,360 --> 00:18:41,300
that a Red Bull would give you, but it's

344
00:18:41,400 --> 00:18:42,440
a traditional mixed drink.

345
00:18:44,800 --> 00:18:47,300
But combining alcohol, which is a depressant,

346
00:18:47,301 --> 00:18:50,600
with a stimulant like caffeine can send mixed messages

347
00:18:50,700 --> 00:18:52,900
to the nervous system and possibly cause

348
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:55,940
cardiac-related problems.

349
00:18:56,040 --> 00:18:57,940
Energy drinks, when you mix them with alcohol,

350
00:18:58,040 --> 00:19:00,340
do give the person who's consuming them

351
00:19:00,440 --> 00:19:04,380
a little bit of a false sense of alertness, of being fine,

352
00:19:04,480 --> 00:19:07,360
and perhaps feeling more sober than they actually are.

353
00:19:07,460 --> 00:19:09,820
And it might cause them to continue drinking when maybe

354
00:19:09,920 --> 00:19:11,760
they shouldn't.

355
00:19:11,860 --> 00:19:14,100
But adding caffeinated beverages to alcohol

356
00:19:14,200 --> 00:19:17,300
is nothing new.

357
00:19:17,301 --> 00:19:18,840
Near the turn of the 20th century,

358
00:19:18,940 --> 00:19:22,280
during the Spanish-American War, American soldiers

359
00:19:22,380 --> 00:19:26,300
took what was then a new beverage, Coke, to Cuba,

360
00:19:26,400 --> 00:19:27,940
where they and others that followed

361
00:19:28,040 --> 00:19:31,120
added rum to make a cocktail that's still popular today.

362
00:19:31,220 --> 00:19:33,520
The key to mixing energy drinks and alcohol

363
00:19:33,620 --> 00:19:34,980
is not to have too much of either.

364
00:19:35,080 --> 00:19:36,360
You shouldn't have too much alcohol,

365
00:19:36,460 --> 00:19:38,480
and you shouldn't have too much of an energy drink.

366
00:19:38,580 --> 00:19:41,320
And you need to realize that too much of either one of them

367
00:19:41,420 --> 00:19:44,620
are going to potentially be harmful.

368
00:19:44,720 --> 00:19:46,360
As energy drinks in the bar scene

369
00:19:46,361 --> 00:19:49,700
continue to raise concerns.

370
00:19:49,800 --> 00:19:52,900
This brewery makes a beer even a child could drink.

371
00:20:00,040 --> 00:20:02,840
This microbrewery in Los Angeles contains equipment

372
00:20:02,940 --> 00:20:06,340
normally used to make beer.

373
00:20:06,440 --> 00:20:11,880
But you won't smell any hops or barley here, only ginger.

374
00:20:11,980 --> 00:20:15,660
This is where Chris Reed makes his version of ginger ale.

375
00:20:15,661 --> 00:20:19,660
using principles dating back more than two centuries.

376
00:20:19,661 --> 00:20:21,160
This is where it starts.

377
00:20:21,161 --> 00:20:23,660
Our company makes ginger ale from real ginger,

378
00:20:23,661 --> 00:20:28,660
and we use about 1.5 million pounds a year of fresh ginger.

379
00:20:28,661 --> 00:20:31,660
The average bottle will have anywhere from 8 grams

380
00:20:31,661 --> 00:20:33,660
to 25 grams of ginger.

381
00:20:33,661 --> 00:20:36,660
This batch here will be for about 700 cases,

382
00:20:36,661 --> 00:20:38,660
and we'll use about 700 pounds.

383
00:20:38,661 --> 00:20:41,660
So we use approximately a pound per case.

384
00:20:41,661 --> 00:20:44,660
That's a lot of ginger.

385
00:20:44,661 --> 00:20:47,560
Fresh ginger, spices, and a whole lot of water

386
00:20:47,660 --> 00:20:53,200
go into this 1,000-gallon steam-jacketed brewing kettle.

387
00:20:53,300 --> 00:20:54,900
It's actually two kettles.

388
00:20:55,000 --> 00:20:58,100
On the outside of the inner kettle is steam,

389
00:20:58,200 --> 00:20:59,600
and that heats up the water.

390
00:20:59,700 --> 00:21:01,620
So we're actually boiling water in there,

391
00:21:01,720 --> 00:21:05,820
and we're throwing the roots right into the boiling water.

392
00:21:05,920 --> 00:21:10,720
Large rotating paddles mix the ginger-infused liquid.

393
00:21:10,820 --> 00:21:13,220
Once brewed, it's strained through a perforated false

394
00:21:13,221 --> 00:21:18,720
bottom, then piped into a separate kettle.

395
00:21:18,820 --> 00:21:21,720
Here, brewers mix in sweeteners and natural flavors.

396
00:21:27,060 --> 00:21:29,540
Chill to 34 degrees Fahrenheit.

397
00:21:29,640 --> 00:21:31,300
The mixture marinates for a few days

398
00:21:31,400 --> 00:21:34,340
before reaching the final step in the brewing process,

399
00:21:34,440 --> 00:21:36,780
carbonation.

400
00:21:36,880 --> 00:21:39,980
Chris uses a centuries-old carbonation process,

401
00:21:40,080 --> 00:21:43,220
still used in the craft beer industry.

402
00:21:43,221 --> 00:21:46,120
A small micro-pore rod slowly feeds CO2

403
00:21:46,220 --> 00:21:48,760
into the 1,800-gallon holding tank.

404
00:21:48,860 --> 00:21:51,660
A modern soda plant will run it in a split second

405
00:21:51,760 --> 00:21:53,460
in a very high-tech piece of equipment.

406
00:21:53,560 --> 00:21:55,660
This takes me 12 hours to carbonate,

407
00:21:55,760 --> 00:21:59,980
but it produces a very small champagne-like carbonation,

408
00:22:00,080 --> 00:22:03,480
which is, for the aficionado, a superior carbonation.

409
00:22:03,580 --> 00:22:07,380
So when we're done, this is like a huge bottle of soda.

410
00:22:07,480 --> 00:22:11,140
After pasteurization, 2,000 champagne-style bottles

411
00:22:11,141 --> 00:22:18,840
bottles of ginger ale per hour are bottled, labeled,

412
00:22:18,940 --> 00:22:20,140
and boxed.

413
00:22:20,240 --> 00:22:22,880
So this is a very big version of the way it was made maybe

414
00:22:22,980 --> 00:22:25,980
in the 1800s or the 1700s.

415
00:22:26,080 --> 00:22:28,360
And of course, we're the only people commercially who

416
00:22:28,460 --> 00:22:30,020
brew a soda that old fashioned way.

417
00:22:30,120 --> 00:22:33,600
That's our claim to fame.

418
00:22:33,700 --> 00:22:36,960
Before commercialization and high-tech carbonation,

419
00:22:37,060 --> 00:22:39,760
soft drinks were the products of home brewers.

420
00:22:39,761 --> 00:22:42,760
Before they had fancy bottling plants to bottle it up

421
00:22:42,860 --> 00:22:46,860
and add carbonation, they would brew up their roots,

422
00:22:46,960 --> 00:22:49,900
and then they would add sugar to sweeten it,

423
00:22:50,000 --> 00:22:52,200
and then they would add yeast, and they would bottle it.

424
00:22:52,300 --> 00:22:54,700
The yeast is in the bottle, eating the sugars,

425
00:22:54,800 --> 00:22:56,520
giving off CO2.

426
00:22:56,620 --> 00:22:58,780
So it's great for carbonation, but it's also

427
00:22:58,880 --> 00:23:01,520
doing what it does in beer, which is produce alcohol.

428
00:23:01,620 --> 00:23:04,620
So your early soft drinks were slightly alcoholic.

429
00:23:07,660 --> 00:23:09,760
Well, before brewers were making bubbles,

430
00:23:09,761 --> 00:23:13,100
Ginger's medicinal properties were known and used.

431
00:23:13,200 --> 00:23:15,640
Ginger has a huge health history.

432
00:23:15,740 --> 00:23:19,040
The Greeks and the Romans, known for their overeating habits,

433
00:23:19,140 --> 00:23:21,780
they would use ginger as the Alka-Seltzer.

434
00:23:21,880 --> 00:23:24,080
So it has quite a tradition of helping people

435
00:23:24,180 --> 00:23:25,580
with digestive problems.

436
00:23:27,520 --> 00:23:29,980
Another root thought to have medicinal properties

437
00:23:30,080 --> 00:23:33,620
comes from the sassafras tree and eventually became

438
00:23:33,720 --> 00:23:37,660
the basis for another iconic soft drink, root beer.

439
00:23:39,760 --> 00:23:42,760
For centuries, Native Americans brewed its roots into their own

440
00:23:42,860 --> 00:23:45,060
brand of tonic.

441
00:23:45,160 --> 00:23:48,800
The root tonic was eventually commercialized in 1876

442
00:23:48,900 --> 00:23:53,200
in Philadelphia, site of America's first World's Fair.

443
00:23:53,300 --> 00:23:57,280
There, pharmacist Charles Hires introduced his blend of root

444
00:23:57,380 --> 00:23:59,420
spiced tea to the paying public.

445
00:24:01,820 --> 00:24:04,460
Root beer became an increasingly popular soft drink flavor

446
00:24:04,560 --> 00:24:09,060
until 1960, when the FDA banned the use of root beer's key

447
00:24:09,061 --> 00:24:14,060
ingredient, sassafras, labeling it a potential carcinogen.

448
00:24:14,160 --> 00:24:17,460
The sassafras root that had all the health benefits

449
